`mergeActionsIntoObjects()` is not idempotent: if a stack has already had actions merged (e.g., `defineStack()` output) and is later passed through `composeStacks()` (or `defineStack()` is called again on an already-parsed stack), the same top-level actions will be appended again to `obj.actions`, producing duplicates. Make the merge step dedupe by action identity (typically `name`) or avoid running the merge in both `composeStacks()` and `defineStack()` for already-normalized inputs; adding a regression test for “defineStack → composeStacks → defineStack” would help prevent reintroducing this.
```suggestion

    // Ensure idempotency: avoid adding duplicate actions when mergeActionsIntoObjects()
    // is called multiple times on an already-normalized stack.
    const existingActions = obj.actions ?? [];
    const existingNames = new Set(existingActions.map((a) => a.name));
    const dedupedNewActions = objActions.filter((a) => !existingNames.has(a.name));

    if (dedupedNewActions.length === 0) {
      return obj;
    }

    return {
      ...obj,
      actions: [...existingActions, ...dedupedNewActions],
```
